  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSaturday into Sunday: By the evening hours, the potent mid-\nlevel wave will be crossing Kansas with 100 meter height falls \nover Missouri. The core of the DCVA and resultant surface low \npressure looks to cross southeastern Missouri. Depending upon \nthe exact track of the surface low, thunder might be possible \nacross our southern zones. Latest model guidance is advertising \nwidespread rainfall of 0.25\" to 0.50\" Saturday night into \nSunday.\n\nMonday through Friday: Low pressure will depart the region with a \ncold front crossing Missouri/ Illinois. Dry conditions will persist \nthrough Wednesday with a slow warming trend starting Tuesday. LREF \nIQRs are only around 5 degrees or so through this period with a \nhigher model confidence than normal. Thursday into Friday IQRs start \nto widen as models advertise the next potent mid-level wave \napproaching from the southwest. Even here though, WPC cluster \nanalysis shows relatively equal clustering between the EPS, GEFS and \nGEPS. This system looks like it will bring another round of \nwidespread precipitation with a cold front crossing the area at the \nend of next work week/ weekend with temperatures falling behind.",
